Fermat’s Method:

Fermat's method is based on the fact that any odd number N = p × q can be written as a difference of two squares, N = a² − b², because a² − b² factors into (a − b)(a + b), so once you find a and b, the factors are simply p = a − b and q = a + b. The code hunts for such a and b by guessing. It starts with a = isqrt(N) + 1, the smallest whole number whose square is at least N (a has to be at least the square root of N, since a² = N + b²). Then, inside a loop, it computes b2 = a*a - N, which is the value b² would have to equal if this a were correct. It takes b = isqrt(b2), the integer square root of that value, and checks if b*b == b2, which is true only when b2 is a perfect square. If it is, we have found a valid pair, and the function returns a - b, a + b as the two factors. If not, a += 1 moves to the next candidate and the loop repeats. For example, with N = 91, a = 10 gives b2 = 9, which is 3², so the factors are 7 and 13. The method is fast only when p and q are close together, because then the correct a sits just above √N and is found within a few steps; when the primes are far apart, the loop could run practically forever, and it also assumes N is odd. 

